	doi = {10.1145/3302389},
	abstract = {The minimum distance of an error-correcting code is an important concept in information theory. Hence, computing the minimum distance of a code with a minimum computational cost is crucial to many problems in this area. In this article, we present and assess a family of implementations of both the brute-force algorithm and the Brouwer-Zimmermann algorithm for computing the minimum distance of a random linear code over F
              2
